.TH TILES 2
.SH NAME
tiles: init, rgb, colour, entry, pal, chr, meta, font, surface, screen, cmd, padkey, edge, sound, sfx, sfxtick, quiet \- an 8-bit console look-alike
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "draw.m";
include "tiles.m";
nes := load Tiles Tiles->PATH;
Chr, Pal, Surface: import tiles;

W, H: con 256, 240;
TW, TH: con 8, 8;
NTW, NTH: con 32, 30;
MASTER: con 64;

PA, PB, PSEL, PSTART, PUP, PDOWN, PLEFT, PRIGHT: con 1 << iota;
FLIPX, FLIPY: con 1 << iota;

GREY, RED, ORANGE, YELLOW, LIME, GREEN, SPRING,
	CYAN, AZURE, BLUE, VIOLET, MAGENTA, ROSE: con iota;
BLACK: con 13;
DARK, MID, LIGHT, PALE: con iota;

init:    fn(): string;
rgb:     fn(i: int): int;
colour:  fn(d: ref Draw->Display, i: int): ref Draw->Image;
entry:   fn(row, col: int): int;
pal:     fn(a, b, c, d: int): ref Pal;
chr:     fn(d: ref Draw->Display, chans: Draw->Chans, scale: int,
              art: array of string): ref Chr;
meta:    fn(art: array of string): array of string;
font:    fn(d: ref Draw->Display, chans: Draw->Chans, scale: int): ref Chr;
surface: fn(d: ref Draw->Display, chr, fnt: ref Chr,
              w, h, bg: int): ref Surface;
padkey:  fn(pad, k: int): int;
edge:    fn(pad, prev: int): int;
sound:   fn(on: int): int;
sfx:     fn(pri, freq, ms: int);
sfxtick: fn();
quiet:   fn();
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tiles
is the graphics of the 8-bit console era, in Limbo:
a fixed 256 by 240 logical screen built from 8 by 8 tiles,
four colours to a tile out of a 64-entry master palette,
drawn through an integer scale so a logical pixel is a square block of
real ones.
It is not emulation.
There is no processor and no picture unit here, and the palette is
computed rather than transcribed from any machine's tables.
What it reproduces is the
.I shape
of that hardware, which is what makes a game written against it look
right, and \- because a frame is a few dozen blits rather than a few
hundred thousand pixels \- what makes one cheap enough to run at 60
frames a second in interpreted Limbo.
.PP
The rule the whole library is built around is that
.I draw
does not resample.
So everything is composited once, up front, at the destination's scale,
and every draw after that is 1:1.
Nothing is scaled per frame.
.SS The palette
.I Init
computes 64 colours as four rows of sixteen.
Column
.B GREY
is a grey, columns
.B RED
through
.B ROSE
are twelve hues, and 13 to 15 are black.
Row
.B DARK
is the shade a sprite is outlined in and row
.B PALE
the near-white a highlight uses.
.I Entry
turns a row and a column into an index,
.I rgb
gives that index as
.BR 0xRRGGBBFF ,
and
.I colour
gives it as a one-pixel replicated
.I Image
suitable for
.IR draw .
.SS Tiles
.I Chr
builds a tile sheet from art written as eight strings of eight
characters a tile, drawn from
.BR ".123" ,
where
.B .
is colour 0.
A sheet therefore reads as itself in the source and needs no asset file.
.PP
.I Meta
takes sixteen strings of sixteen characters \- which is how a 16 by 16
character actually wants to be written \- and cuts each such block into
the four tiles
.I chr
wants, in the order top left, top right, bottom left, bottom right.
Concatenate its result onto an array of hand-written 8 by 8 tiles to get
a sheet with both.
.PP
.I Font
returns a 256-tile sheet of the 8 by 8 text font, read from
.BR /fonts/temple/temple.8x8 ,
which is already in the tree and already the right shape.
Glyph
.I i
is tile
.IR i ,
so a string indexes it directly.
.PP
A
.I Pal
is four master-palette indices.
.B c[0]
is the background colour for
.I Surface.tile
and transparent for
.IR Surface.put .
Composites are built lazily, one per tile, palette and flip, and only
for the combinations a game actually draws:
a 96-tile sheet at four palettes is 1536 of them and a game touches a
few dozen.
.SS Surfaces
.I Surface
wraps an image whose size is
.I scale
times the logical
.I w
by
.IR h .
.I Tile
draws an opaque tile at tile coordinates and
.I put
a masked one at pixel coordinates;
.I put16
draws a 16 by 16 metasprite, and its
.B FLIPX
swaps the halves as well as mirroring them.
.I Fill
paints a logical rectangle in a master colour and
.I text
writes a string in the font.
.PP
A game keeps two: one the size of the screen to compose each frame
into, and one the size of the level, built once.
.I View
copies a screen-sized window of the second into the first \- that is the
scroll \- and
.I blit
copies the whole of one into the other.
.I View
clamps its origin so the window stays inside the source, because
.I draw
replicates a source smaller than its destination rather than stopping at
the edge, and a level that tiled itself at the end would look like a
feature.
.SS Input
.I Padkey
takes one key event and the current pad and returns the new pad, so a
game keeps no key state of its own.
It handles the release as well as the press, which means the window must
have asked for
.B keyup
as well as
.B kbd
\- see
.IR wmclient (2)
\-
or nothing is ever released and every key sticks down.
Both arrow keys and
.B wasd
work the pad;
.BR z ,
.B ,
are
.BR PB ;
.BR x ,
.B .
and space are
.BR PA .
.PP
.I Edge
returns the buttons in
.I pad
that were not in
.IR prev :
what was pressed since the last frame.
Games use it for jump and fire and the raw pad for walking.
.SS Sound
.IR Tone (2)
plays one thing at a time, so
.I sfx
is an arbiter rather than a mixer: an effect states a priority and how
long it lasts, and a quieter one asked for while a louder one is still
sounding is dropped rather than cutting it off.
.I Sfxtick
must be called once a frame for that to expire.
.I Sound
turns audio on or off and returns whether it is on;
.I quiet
silences whatever is playing.
.SS The same thing as a command language
Everything above is Limbo: a game compiles its art in as arrays of
strings and its palettes as calls.
That is fine, and it is the only way to do it, which is the complaint.
.PP
A
.I Screen
is the other way, and it is shaped like
.IR tk (2):
named palettes, named sheets and named surfaces, made and drawn by text,
so a level, a palette and a sheet of art are
.I data
a program reads rather than source a program is built from.
.I Cmd
takes one command or several separated by newlines and returns a result;
an error comes back beginning with
.BR ! ,
which is
.IR tk (2)'s
convention rather than
.IR plot (2)'s
nil-or-error, because a command that answers a question has to be able
to answer
.BR 0 .
.PP
.EX
palette NAME c0 c1 c2 c3     colours as ROW.COLUMN or a master index
sheet NAME FILE              8x8 art, eight lines a tile
sheet16 NAME FILE            16x16 art, sixteen lines each
font NAME                    the built-in 8x8 text font
surface NAME W H COLOUR      W and H in logical pixels
tile SURF TX TY SHEET N PAL
put SURF X Y SHEET N PAL [flipx] [flipy]
put16 SURF X Y SHEET N PAL [flipx] [flipy]
text SURF X Y PAL WORDS...
fill SURF X Y W H COLOUR
view SRC DST SX SY
blit SRC DST X Y
map SURF SHEET FILE
at SURF X Y                  \-> the map character in that cell
set SURF X Y CHAR            repaint one cell from the legend
size SURF                    \-> "W H"
colour NAME                  \-> "#rrggbb"
count SHEET                  \-> how many tiles
.EE
.PP
.I Named ,
.I sheet
and
.I pal
reach a thing made by text from Limbo, so a program can build its screen
from files and then go on calling the functions above on it.
.SS The file formats
An art file is lines of
.BR ".123" ,
eight to a tile for
.BR sheet ,
sixteen for
.BR sheet16 .
A map file carries its own legend, so a level is one file:
.IP
.EX
; a comment
= 2 turf
# 1 brick
%
\&....####....
\&============
.EE
.PP
Lines of
.B "CHAR TILE PALETTE"
until a line of
.BR % ,
then the map, one character a tile.
A character with no legend entry paints nothing, which is how a map says
sky without naming it.
.PP
A map is
.I remembered
as well as painted, which is what lets a program on the far side of this
language play rather than only draw:
.B at
asks what is in a cell and
.B set
changes one and repaints it.
Without those a script can put a maze on the screen and has no way to
ask whether the thing it is about to walk into is a wall.
.IR Warren (1)
is a game that is nothing but those two questions and a few draws.
.PP
A comment begins with
.BR ; ,
not with
.BR # .
That is not a style choice:
.B #
is the obvious character for a wall, and when it was the comment marker
the legend line
.B "# 1 brick"
was thrown away as a comment and every brick in a map silently failed to
draw.
A format whose comment marker collides with its own data is a bad
format.
.PP
Blank lines are dropped from an art file, where they are meaningless and
let a sheet be laid out to be read, and kept in the grid of a map, where
a blank line is a blank row.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/tiles.b
